.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt CUT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cut
.Nd remove sections from each line of files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm cut
.Fl b Ar list
.Op Fl Fl complement
.Op Fl z
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm cut
.Fl c Ar list
.Op Fl Fl complement
.Op Fl z
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm cut
.Fl f Ar list
.Op Fl d Ar delim
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl Fl complement
.Op Fl Fl output-delimiter Ns = Ns Ar string
.Op Fl z
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility cuts out selected portions of each line of each input
.Ar file
(or standard input if no files are specified or when
.Ar file
is
.Dq - )
and writes them to the standard output.
.Pp
Exactly one of the
.Fl b , c ,
or
.Fl f
options must be specified.
The items specified by
.Ar list
refer to byte positions, character positions, or fields,
respectively.
.Pp
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl b Ar list , Fl Fl bytes Ns = Ns Ar list
Select only the bytes in positions specified by
.Ar list .
.It Fl c Ar list , Fl Fl characters Ns = Ns Ar list
Select only the characters in positions specified by
.Ar list .
In the current implementation, this is equivalent to
.Fl b .
.It Fl d Ar delim , Fl Fl delimiter Ns = Ns Ar delim
Use
.Ar delim
as the field delimiter character instead of the tab character.
Only valid with
.Fl f .
.It Fl f Ar list , Fl Fl fields Ns = Ns Ar list
Select only the fields in positions specified by
.Ar list .
Fields are separated by the delimiter character (tab by default).
Lines that do not contain the delimiter are passed through unmodified,
unless the
.Fl s
option is specified.
.It Fl s , Fl Fl only-delimited
Suppress lines that do not contain the field delimiter character.
Only valid with
.Fl f .
.It Fl Fl complement
Complement the set of selected bytes, characters, or fields.
Output consists of the portions
.Em not
selected by the
.Fl b , c ,
or
.Fl f
options.
.It Fl Fl output-delimiter Ns = Ns Ar string
Use
.Ar string
as the output field delimiter instead of the input delimiter.
.It Fl z , Fl Fl zero-terminated
Use NUL as the line delimiter instead of newline.
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Display help and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl Fl version
Output version information and exit.
.El
.Pp
The
.Ar list
argument is a comma-separated list of ranges, each of which is one of:
.Bl -tag -width "N-M"
.It Ar N
The
.Ar N Ns th
byte, character, or field, counted from 1.
.It Ar N Ns -
From the
.Ar N Ns th
byte, character, or field to the end of the line.
.It Ar N Ns - Ns Ar M
From the
.Ar N Ns th
to the
.Ar M Ns th
byte, character, or field, inclusive.
.It - Ns Ar M
From the first to the
.Ar M Ns th
byte, character, or field.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std cut
.Sh EXAMPLES
Extract the first three characters of each line:
.Pp
.Dl "cut -c 1-3 file.txt"
.Pp
Extract the second field from a colon-delimited file:
.Pp
.Dl "cut -d: -f2 /etc/passwd"
.Pp
Extract multiple fields from a CSV-like file:
.Pp
.Dl "cut -d, -f1,3,5 data.csv"
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr awk 1 ,
.Xr paste 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is compliant with the
.St -p1003.1-2008
specification.
.Pp
The
.Fl Fl complement ,
.Fl Fl output-delimiter ,
and
.Fl z
options are GNU extensions.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
